/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Maths (batch z) */
WB.L("pigeonhole-principle", "maths", "beginner", "The pigeonhole principle: the simplest idea that proves surprising things",
  "If you have more pigeons than holes, some hole holds two pigeons. That is the whole idea, and it can prove that two people in Sydney have exactly the same number of hairs.",
  [
    ["Too many pigeons", "If more items go into fewer boxes, some box must hold two, which proves something exists without finding it.",
      "Ten pigeons fly home to nine pigeonholes. However they arrange themselves, at least one hole must hold two birds. There is no trick, no probability and no calculation. It cannot be otherwise. This is the pigeonhole principle, perhaps the most obvious statement in all of mathematics.\n\nMathematicians love it because it proves something must exist without ever finding it. You need not know which hole is crowded, only that one is. Peter Gustav Lejeune Dirichlet used the idea in the 1830s and 1840s, and in German it became the Schubfachprinzip, the drawer principle. The English name probably refers to small desk or mail-sorter compartments, not actual birds."],
    ["Socks, birthdays and hair", "When items outnumber the possible categories, a match is certain, even if you can never find it.",
      "Black and grey socks sit jumbled in a dark drawer. How many must you grab to be certain of a pair? Three. Two socks might be one of each, but a third must match one. The colours are the holes and the socks are the pigeons. Likewise, among 367 people two must share a birthday, since there are only 366 dates. Certainty differs from likelihood: a shared birthday is likely with 23 people, but only 367 guarantees it.\n\nSydney has over five million people. A head holds roughly 100,000 hairs, perhaps up to 150,000, and nobody has a million. Make a hole for every count from zero to one million, and at least two Sydneysiders must have exactly the same number. In fact at least five share one."],
    ["When boxes overflow", "Divide the items by the boxes and round up to find the fewest that must share a box.",
      "A stronger version tells you how crowded a box must be. Suppose 100 people at a conference are each born on one of the seven days of the week. Dividing 100 by 7 gives about 14.3, so at least one weekday is the birthday of 15 or more of them.\n\nThe rule is to divide the number of items by the number of boxes and round up. With 10 pigeons and 3 holes, some hole has at least 4. This is how you can prove that among any 13 people, at least two were born in the same month, and among any 25, at least three were."],
    ["The handshake puzzle", "The pigeonhole trick is to show there are fewer real options than there appear to be.",
      "At a party, guests shake hands with some others, never with themselves or twice with the same person. Prove that two guests shook exactly the same number of hands.\n\nSay there are 20 guests. Each person's count could be anything from 0 to 19, which looks like 20 values for 20 people, just enough to all differ. But look closer. If someone shook nobody's hand, then nobody can have shaken all 19 others. So 0 and 19 cannot both appear. That leaves only 19 usable holes for 20 guests, and two people must share a count. The trick is common: first show there are fewer real options than there seem."],
    ["Limits and habits of mind", "Counting possibilities against items shows what is impossible, from universal compression to collision-free fingerprints.",
      "The principle sets hard limits in computing. Could a clever program shrink every possible file, even by one bit? Counting says no. There are 1,024 different files exactly 10 bits long but only 1,023 shorter ones, including the empty file. If every 10-bit file shrank, two would share a compressed version and the program could not know which to restore. Real compression works because everyday files are full of patterns; truly random data cannot be squeezed.\n\nThe same logic explains why hash functions, which turn any data into a short fingerprint, must sometimes give two inputs the same fingerprint. Security depends on making such collisions extraordinarily hard to find. The principle guarantees only that a match exists, not where it is."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What can the pigeonhole principle prove?", ["How many boxes are crowded","That some box must be shared","Which specific box holds two items","The probability of a shared box"], 1, "It guarantees that some box is shared, even though it says nothing about which one."],
    [1, "Why must two Sydney residents have exactly the same number of hairs?", ["Hair counts are always whole thousands","Most people have the same hair colour","Residents outnumber the possible hair counts","Hair counts never change over time"], 2, "With more than five million people and only about a million possible counts, some count must repeat."],
    [2, "If 10 pigeons go into 3 holes, what can you say for certain?", ["Some hole has at least 4 pigeons","Every hole has exactly 3 pigeons","No hole has more than 3 pigeons","Some hole is left empty"], 0, "Ten divided by three is about 3.3, and rounding up means some hole holds at least 4."],
    [3, "In the 20-guest handshake puzzle, why are there only 19 usable counts?", ["Guests cannot shake their own hands","Nobody shakes hands more than once","Counts of 0 and 19 cannot both occur","Two guests always shake no hands"], 2, "Someone with 19 handshakes met everyone, so nobody can have 0, which leaves fewer options than guests."],
    [4, "Why can no lossless program shrink every possible file?", ["Shorter files are fewer than longer ones","Programs cannot read files bit by bit","Shorter files are always less accurate","Compression only works on text files"], 0, "There are not enough shorter files to give each longer file its own compressed version."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two people in Sydney have exactly the same number of hairs. Here's how we know."});

WB.L("paper-sizes-root-two", "maths", "beginner", "The clever maths hiding in A4 paper",
  "Fold an A4 sheet in half and you get a smaller sheet of exactly the same shape. That is no accident: it rests on a single number, the square root of 2.",
  [
    ["The shape that survives", "Only a rectangle with sides in the ratio of the square root of 2 keeps its shape every time it is halved.",
      "Cut most rectangles in half and the halves change shape. A square becomes two long strips; a sheet twice as long as wide becomes two squares. That is a nuisance for printers, because pages will not shrink neatly onto the next size down.\n\nCall the short side 1 and the long side r. Fold the sheet and the old short side becomes the new long side, while the new short side is r divided by 2. For the shape to stay the same, r over 1 must equal 1 over r/2. So r squared is 2, and r is the square root of 2, about 1.414. A4 measures 210 by 297 millimetres, and 297 divided by 210 is 1.414. Fold it for A5, 148 by 210, with the same proportions."],
    ["Starting from a square metre", "Each size in the A series has half the area of the one before, so enlarging or reducing scales each side by the square root of 2.",
      "A0 has an area of one square metre, measuring 841 by 1,189 millimetres. Halve it for A1, then A2, A3 and A4, which is one sixteenth of a square metre. So a standard 80 gram sheet of A4 weighs 5 grams, handy for estimating the weight of a letter.\n\nThe photocopier's odd settings, 141% and 71%, are the square root of 2 in disguise. To enlarge A4 to A3 the area must double, so each side grows by 1.414, or 141%. Shrinking A4 to A5 scales each side by 0.707, or 71%. Because every sheet has the same shape, copies fit with no white strip down one side."],
    ["A two-century idea", "The square root of 2 shape was noticed in 1786, but took until the twentieth century to become an international standard.",
      "In 1786 the German scientist Georg Christoph Lichtenberg noted in a letter that a sheet with sides in the ratio of the square root of 2 could be halved again and again without changing shape. Revolutionary France, busy rationalising everything from weights to calendars, used the same ratio for some official paper in a 1798 stamp duty law.\n\nThe idea then faded until the German engineer Walter Porstmann built a full system around it, starting from one square metre. Germany adopted it as DIN 476 in 1922, and in 1975 it became the international standard ISO 216. Australia uses it, as do most nations. The United States and Canada mostly keep older inch-based sizes such as Letter."],
    ["Envelopes and in-between sizes", "The B and C series fit between the A sizes, so a whole family of sheets and envelopes fits together.",
      "The system has two companion series. B sizes sit between A sizes. B4, at 250 by 353 millimetres, is larger than A4 but smaller than A3, and each side is the geometric mean of its neighbours: multiply them and take the square root. B sizes suit posters, passports and some books.\n\nThe C series, mostly for envelopes, sits between A and B, so a C envelope is just larger than the matching A sheet. A C4 envelope holds an A4 sheet flat, while a C5 takes A4 folded once. The popular DL envelope, 110 by 220 millimetres, suits A4 folded into thirds. One number holds the whole family together."],
    ["Why not the golden ratio?", "Only the square root of 2 survives halving, which is why paper uses it rather than the golden ratio.",
      "People often assume elegant rectangles follow the golden ratio, about 1.618, though many claims of it in art and architecture are exaggerated. A golden rectangle has its own neat trick: cut a square off one end and what remains is another golden rectangle.\n\nThat is beautiful mathematics, but it is not what paper needs. When you fold a sheet you halve it, rather than trim off a square, and only the square root of 2 survives halving. US Letter, 8.5 by 11 inches, has a ratio of about 1.29, and folding it in half changes its shape noticeably. A4 is a quiet reminder that a simple equation, solved properly, can make countless everyday jobs easier."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the ratio of the long side to the short side of A4 paper?", ["About 1.618","About 1.414","About 1.29","About 2.0"], 1, "The square root of 2, about 1.414, is the only ratio that stays the same when a sheet is halved."],
    [1, "Why does a photocopier use 141% to enlarge A4 onto A3?", ["Doubling the area scales each side by 1.414","The paper must be one and a half times longer","Each side must double in length","Copiers always add a 41% margin"], 0, "Doubling the area means scaling each side by the square root of 2, about 141%."],
    [2, "What did Lichtenberg notice about sheets with sides in the ratio of the square root of 2?", ["They halve again and again without changing shape","They were the most pleasing rectangle","They always had an area of one square metre","They fitted any envelope without folding"], 0, "Because halving preserves the shape, such sheets can be divided again and again."],
    [3, "Which envelope is designed for A4 folded into thirds?", ["C4","C5","B4","DL"], 3, "The DL envelope, 110 by 220 millimetres, is made for an A4 sheet folded in three."],
    [4, "Why does paper use the square root of 2 rather than the golden ratio?", ["The golden ratio cannot be measured","Only the square root of 2 survives halving","Golden rectangles are too narrow to print","The golden ratio is only used in the United States"], 1, "A sheet is halved, not trimmed by a square, and only the square root of 2 keeps its shape when halved."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Fold A4 in half and the shape stays the same. One number makes that possible."});

WB.L("shuffled-deck-factorial", "maths", "beginner", "Every shuffle is history: the astonishing size of 52!",
  "Shuffle a pack of cards properly and you have almost certainly created an order that no one in history has ever held. The maths behind that claim is simple and staggering.",
  [
    ["Fifty-two factorial", "A pack of 52 cards can be ordered in about 8 followed by 67 zeros ways, far more than all shuffles ever made.",
      "Three books can be arranged 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 ways; four, 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 24. This multiplying-down pattern is a factorial, written 4!. Christian Kramp introduced the notation in 1808. Ten books give 3,628,800 orders, and twenty give more than two quintillion.\n\nFor a pack of 52 cards, 52! is roughly 8 × 10⁶⁷, a number 68 digits long that begins 8065. Imagine all eight billion people alive today shuffling once a second since the Big Bang, about 13.8 billion years ago. They would have made roughly 3.5 × 10²⁷ decks, less than a billionth of a billionth of a billionth of all possible orders."],
    ["Almost certainly a first", "A thoroughly shuffled deck is almost certainly in a new order, and about seven riffle shuffles are enough to get there.",
      "A well-shuffled deck is almost certainly in an order that has never existed before. The catch is the word ‘well’. A new pack comes in set order, and a couple of lazy shuffles leave much of it intact. Uniqueness applies only when all 52! orders are equally likely.\n\nHow many shuffles is enough? In 1992 Dave Bayer and Persi Diaconis analysed the riffle shuffle, where you split the deck and let the halves fall together. The deck stays noticeably ordered at first, then becomes well mixed quite suddenly at around seven shuffles. More adds little. The overhand shuffle is far weaker: one analysis suggested thousands. Diaconis left home as a teenager to travel as a magician before becoming a professor of statistics."],
    ["The perfect shuffle", "A perfectly precise shuffle is not random, and repeating a fixed rearrangement eventually returns you to the start.",
      "Skilled magicians can cut a deck into two exact halves of 26 and interleave them perfectly, one card from each side in turn. In the out-shuffle, the top card stays on top. Do it once and the deck looks mixed. Keep going and something surprising happens: after exactly eight perfect out-shuffles, a 52-card deck returns to its original order.\n\nMagicians use this predictability to make a pack look mixed while knowing where every card is. The lesson is that mixing depends on randomness. A shuffle that is too precise is just a permutation, a fixed rearrangement, and repeating a fixed rearrangement enough times brings you back to where you began."],
    ["When order doesn't matter", "Divide by the number of ways to rearrange a selection when the order of its items makes no difference.",
      "Sometimes order does not matter. A five-card poker hand is the same hand whatever order you pick the cards up in. To count hands, start with 52 × 51 × 50 × 49 × 48 ordered ways to deal five cards, then divide by 5!, the 120 ways of rearranging the same five. That gives 2,598,960 possible hands.\n\nThe same idea measures lotteries. A game where you choose 6 numbers from 45 has 8,145,060 combinations, so one entry has about a one in eight million chance of the top prize. This kind of counting is called combinatorics. It tells you plainly how long the odds are, and why a lottery ticket is entertainment, not a plan."],
    ["Why big numbers protect us", "When you want something hard to guess, length and genuine randomness matter far more than clever substitutions.",
      "Explosive growth in possibilities protects you every day. A four-digit PIN has only 10,000 possibilities, which is why banks lock a card after a few wrong guesses. A long password made of several random words has vastly more, because each extra word multiplies the total.\n\nModern encryption relies on keys chosen from so many possibilities that trying them all would take longer than the age of the universe, even with enormous computing power. The same principle that makes a shuffled deck unique makes a properly chosen key unguessable.\n\nThe practical takeaway: length and genuine randomness matter far more than clever substitutions, such as swapping the letter o for a zero."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does a pack of 52 cards have so many possible orders?", ["Choices shrink: 52, then 51, then 50 …","Each card can face up or down","Cards can be dealt in any of 52 suits","Every shuffle adds a new card"], 0, "Multiplying the shrinking number of choices for each position gives 52!, an enormous total."],
    [1, "Why is a well-shuffled deck almost certainly in a unique order?", ["Casinos never reuse a deck","Possible orders vastly outnumber shuffles ever made","Every shuffle changes the order of the cards","Cards keep their order after any shuffle"], 1, "The number of possible orders dwarfs the number of shuffles that have taken place."],
    [2, "What happens after eight perfect out-shuffles of a 52-card deck?", ["The deck is fully random","The deck is completely reversed","The deck returns to its original order","The deck ends in alternating colours"], 2, "A perfect shuffle is a fixed rearrangement, so repeating it enough times cycles back to the start."],
    [3, "Why divide by 5! when counting poker hands?", ["The order of the five cards does not matter","Five cards are dealt face down","Only five suits are used","Hands with matching suits are removed"], 0, "Each hand can be picked up in 120 orders, so dividing removes the repeats."],
    [4, "What makes a password or key hard to guess?", ["Swapping letters for similar-looking numbers","Using a familiar phrase with a capital letter","Length and genuine randomness","Changing it every day"], 2, "Each extra random element multiplies the possibilities, while predictable substitutions add little."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your last shuffle probably produced an order that has never existed before. Here's why."});

WB.L("average-speed-trap", "maths", "beginner", "The averaging trap: when rates don't add up",
  "Drive somewhere at 60 km/h and back at 40, and your average speed is not 50. Averages of rates fool almost everyone, and the mistake shows up in fuel bills, class sizes and bus stops.",
  [
    ["The return trip puzzle", "Average speed is total distance divided by total time, so averaging the speeds themselves gives the wrong answer.",
      "You drive 60 km to a friend at 60 km/h, then crawl home through roadworks at 40 km/h. Your average speed is not 50 km/h. The trip there took one hour. The trip home took 1.5 hours. You covered 120 km in 2.5 hours, which is 48 km/h. The slow leg counts for more because you spend longer on it. Averaging the speeds gives both legs equal weight.\n\nFor equal distances, the proper tool is the harmonic mean: 2 divided by (1/60 + 1/40) is 48. It is never above the ordinary average and leans towards the smaller number. The simple rule: add up the totals, then divide, rather than averaging the rates."],
    ["The impossible hill", "Translate rates back into real quantities such as distance and time before averaging anything.",
      "A cyclist rides one kilometre up a steep hill at 30 km/h and wants to average 60 km/h over the two-kilometre round trip. How fast must she go downhill? Intuition says 90 km/h, to balance the 30.\n\nBut to average 60 km/h over two kilometres, the whole trip must take two minutes. The climb alone, one kilometre at 30 km/h, has already used those two minutes. She would have to descend in no time at all, which means infinite speed. The target is impossible. Puzzles like this show why it pays to turn rates back into distances and times before averaging."],
    ["The fuel economy illusion", "Miles per gallon hides savings: swapping a thirsty car for a modest one saves more fuel than upgrading an efficient one.",
      "In 2008 Richard Larrick and Jack Soll published a study in Science on the ‘MPG illusion’. People assume that a bigger improvement in miles per gallon saves more fuel. Australia mostly uses litres per 100 kilometres, which avoids the trap.\n\nCompare two upgrades. Replacing a car that uses 20 litres per 100 kilometres with one using 15 saves 5 litres every 100 kilometres. Replacing an efficient car using 6 litres with one using 4 saves only 2. In kilometres per litre, the first goes from 5 to about 6.7, while the second leaps from about 16.7 to 25. The second looks far more impressive, yet the first saves more than twice as much fuel."],
    ["Who is doing the counting", "Averages depend on who is counting: people tend to land in the bigger classes and the longer gaps.",
      "A small college has two classes, one of 10 students and one of 90. It can truthfully report an average class size of 50. But ask each student. Ten answer 10 and ninety answer 90, which averages 82. Both are correct, yet most students sit in the crowded class. The friendship paradox is similar: on average, your friends have more friends than you do, because popular people appear in many friend lists.\n\nBuses average one every 10 minutes, but suppose gaps alternate between 5 and 15 minutes. A random arrival is three times as likely to land in a 15-minute gap. Your expected wait is 6.25 minutes, not 5. This is the inspection paradox: longer things are more likely to be the ones you find."],
    ["Habits that beat the trap", "An average only means something if you know what it is an average of.",
      "These traps share a cause: an average is only meaningful if you know what it is an average of. Three questions protect you.\n\nFirst, are you averaging rates? If so, go back to the totals, such as distance and time, or fuel and kilometres, and divide those instead.\n\nSecond, who is doing the counting? An average across classes, buses or businesses can differ wildly from the average experienced by the people inside them.\n\nThird, would a different unit tell a different story? Kilometres per litre and litres per 100 kilometres carry identical information, but one makes savings easy to see. None of this needs advanced maths, just the patience to ask what lies behind a number before trusting it."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "You drive 60 km at 60 km/h and return the same 60 km at 40 km/h. What is your average speed?", ["50 km/h","48 km/h","52 km/h","45 km/h"], 1, "You cover 120 km in 2.5 hours, and the slower leg takes more of the time."],
    [1, "Why can a cyclist never average 60 km/h over a 2 km round trip after climbing 1 km at 30 km/h?", ["Downhill speed cannot exceed 90 km/h","Climbing always costs twice as much time","The climb alone uses up all the time allowed","Round trips must be measured in whole minutes"], 2, "At 60 km/h the whole trip takes two minutes, and the climb has already used them."],
    [2, "Which car swap saves more fuel over 100 km?", ["From 20 to 15 litres per 100 km","From 6 to 4 litres per 100 km","Both save exactly the same","Neither saves any fuel"], 0, "The first saves 5 litres and the second only 2, even though the second looks bigger in km per litre."],
    [3, "A college has classes of 10 and 90 students. What class size does the average student experience?", ["50","10","82","90"], 2, "Most students sit in the large class, so their typical experience is far more crowded than the official average."],
    [4, "What should you do first when you need to average rates?", ["Average the rates directly","Take the highest of the rates","Round each rate to a tidy number","Go back to the totals and divide"], 3, "Dividing total distance by total time, or total fuel by total distance, avoids giving unequal legs equal weight."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Drive there at 60 and back at 40, and your average isn't 50. Find out why."});

WB.L("doomsday-rule", "maths", "beginner", "The doomsday rule: find the weekday of any date in your head",
  "A mathematician named John Conway could tell you the day of the week for any date in history in a couple of seconds. His method is easy to learn, and it starts with 4 April.",
  [
    ["A grim party trick", "In any year, a set of memorable dates share one weekday, called the doomsday, which anchors every other date.",
      "In the early 1970s the British mathematician John Horton Conway, famous for inventing the Game of Life, devised a way to work out the weekday of any date mentally. He built on an earlier method by Lewis Carroll, the author of Alice's Adventures in Wonderland, who was also a mathematician.\n\nThe trick rests on a striking fact. In any given year, a whole collection of memorable dates always land on the same weekday. Conway called that weekday the year's ‘doomsday’. The name is not sinister. It simply means the anchor day for that year."],
    ["The anchor dates", "Memorable anchor dates always share the doomsday weekday, so any date is only a short count away.",
      "The even months are easy: 4 April, 6 June, 8 August, 10 October and 12 December (4/4, 6/6, 8/8, 10/10, 12/12). For the odd months, the mnemonic ‘I work 9 to 5 at 7-Eleven’ gives 9 May, 5 September, 11 July and 7 November. The last day of February is always a doomsday, as is 14 March. For January, use 3 January in ordinary years and 4 January in leap years.\n\nIn 2026 all of these are Saturdays. Christmas? The nearest anchor is 12 December, a Saturday, so 19 and 26 December are too, making Christmas a Friday. Australia Day: 3 January is Saturday, so 31 January is too, making 26 January a Monday."],
    ["Moving from year to year", "Each ordinary year moves the doomsday forward one weekday and each leap year two, starting from a century anchor.",
      "A normal year has 365 days, which is 52 weeks and one day, so the doomsday moves forward by one weekday. A leap year adds a day, so the jump after 29 February is two. In 2026 the doomsday is Saturday. In 2027 it is Sunday. In 2028, a leap year, it jumps two places to Tuesday.\n\nTo handle any year in a century, start from a known anchor: Wednesday for the 1900s and Tuesday for the 2000s. Add the number of years past the century, plus the number of leap years in that stretch, and count forward that many weekdays, ignoring complete weeks."],
    ["A worked example", "Weekdays repeat every seven days, so only the remainder after removing complete weeks matters.",
      "Test the method on Neil Armstrong's Moon step on 20 July 1969, by American time. The 1900s anchor is Wednesday. The year is 69 years into the century, which includes 17 leap years, since 69 divided by 4 is 17 with some left over. Add them: 69 plus 17 is 86.\n\nRemove complete weeks: 86 divided by 7 is 12 weeks with 2 left over. Two days on from Wednesday makes 1969's doomsday a Friday. The July anchor is 11 July, a Friday, and so is 18 July. That makes 20 July a Sunday. In eastern Australia it was already the next day, so viewers here watched on Monday 21 July."],
    ["Why it works", "The Gregorian calendar repeats exactly every 400 years, and weekday cycles are modular arithmetic, the arithmetic of remainders.",
      "The method works because the calendar follows fixed rules. Pope Gregory XIII introduced the Gregorian calendar in 1582. A year is a leap year if divisible by 4, except century years, which must be divisible by 400. So 1900 was not a leap year but 2000 was. The 400-year cycle holds 146,097 days, exactly 20,871 weeks, so the calendar repeats perfectly, and the 13th falls on a Friday slightly more often than any other weekday.\n\nConway reportedly had his computer quiz him with random dates when he logged in. He died in April 2020. To learn the method, start with the current year's anchors. It teaches modular arithmetic: weekdays cycle every seven, as hours cycle every twelve."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a year's ‘doomsday’?", ["The weekday shared by 4/4, 6/6 and 8/8","The final Saturday in each year","The weekday on which 1 January falls","The weekday that 29 February falls on"], 0, "Memorable dates in a year all share one weekday, which anchors every other date."],
    [1, "If 12 December 2026 is a Saturday, what day is 25 December 2026?", ["Thursday","Friday","Saturday","Sunday"], 1, "Add two weeks to reach 26 December, a Saturday, then Christmas is the day before."],
    [2, "Why does the doomsday jump from Sunday in 2027 to Tuesday in 2028?", ["2028 is a leap year, so it moves two days","The 2028 calendar starts on a Tuesday","Doomsdays always move by two days","Calendars are reset every decade"], 0, "A leap year adds 29 February, which pushes the doomsday on by two weekdays instead of one."],
    [3, "Why can you remove complete weeks in the Moon landing calculation?", ["Weekdays repeat every seven days","Weeks are always 86 days long","Leap years only count whole weeks","Each year has exactly 52 weeks"], 0, "Because weekdays cycle every seven days, 86 days forward is the same as 2 days forward."],
    [4, "Why does the Gregorian calendar repeat every 400 years?", ["Every century year is a leap year","Pope Gregory fixed the weekdays","400 years hold a whole number of weeks","Leap years only happen every 400 years"], 2, "146,097 days is exactly 20,871 weeks, so every date lands on the same weekday after 400 years."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Name any date in history and the weekday follows in seconds, starting from 4 April."});

WB.L("napier-logarithms", "maths", "intermediate", "Logarithms: the invention that turned multiplying into adding",
  "In 1614 a Scottish nobleman published tables that saved astronomers years of drudgery. For the next 350 years, his idea sat in the pocket of every engineer as the slide rule.",
  [
    ["The drudgery of multiplying", "Adding is easy and multiplying is hard, and patterns in powers hint at a way to turn one into the other.",
      "Picture an astronomer around 1600 working out a planet's orbit. Each step means multiplying and dividing numbers of seven or more digits, and one slip ruins weeks of work. Navigators and surveyors faced the same burden. Adding large numbers is quick and fairly safe, but multiplying by hand means rows of partial products and lots of carrying.\n\nMathematicians wondered whether hard multiplication could become easy addition. A clue came from powers. Since 2 × 2 × 2 is 8 and 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 is 16, multiplying 8 by 16 means adding the counts of twos, 3 plus 4, to get 7 twos. That is 128."],
    ["Napier's idea", "A logarithm is a power, so adding logarithms multiplies numbers and subtracting them divides.",
      "John Napier was the Laird of Merchiston near Edinburgh, who also wrote a fiercely argued book on the Book of Revelation. Mathematics was his passion. He spent around two decades developing logarithms, a word built from Greek roots meaning ratio and number, and published them in 1614. His tables paired numbers with their logarithms. Napier's version differs from today's, but the essential trick was there.\n\nWith base 10, the logarithm of 100 is 2 and of 1,000 is 3. The logarithm of 2 is about 0.301 and of 8 about 0.903. Add them to get 1.204, look up the number with that logarithm, and you find 16, which is 2 × 8. Division becomes subtraction, powers become multiplication, and square roots become halving."],
    ["Briggs and Kepler", "Base 10 tables and Kepler's planetary tables put logarithms to work across science.",
      "The English mathematician Henry Briggs travelled to Scotland in 1615 to meet Napier. They agreed that logarithms would be more convenient in base 10, so the logarithm of 10 was 1 and of 100 was 2. Napier died in 1617. Briggs calculated the new tables by hand, including a large volume in 1624. These common logarithms stayed in classrooms, including Australian ones, until cheap electronic calculators arrived in the 1970s.\n\nThe astronomer Johannes Kepler seized on logarithms early. He used them in completing the Rudolphine Tables, published in 1627, which predicted planetary positions far more accurately than anything before. Logarithms did not create the scientific revolution, but they made its endless calculations bearable."],
    ["The slide rule", "Sliding two logarithmic scales against each other adds lengths, which multiplies numbers.",
      "Soon after Napier, the English mathematician Edmund Gunter drew a ruler marked with a logarithmic scale, so distances along it represented logarithms. Using dividers, users could add lengths and so multiply numbers. In the 1620s and 1630s the clergyman William Oughtred slid two such scales against each other, which did the adding automatically. This was the slide rule.\n\nFor centuries it was the engineer's constant companion, helping design bridges, ships, aircraft and, at NASA, the rockets of the Apollo programme. A good slide rule gave about three significant figures, accurate enough for most engineering. Pocket calculators replaced it almost overnight in the 1970s."],
    ["Logarithms today", "Logarithms are the natural way to measure quantities that span huge ranges or grow by multiplying.",
      "Calculators made log tables obsolete, but logarithms are everywhere. The decibel scale for sound is logarithmic: every 10 decibels means ten times the sound intensity. Earthquake magnitude scales are logarithmic, as is the pH scale, where each step is a tenfold change. In finance and biology, logarithms describe compound growth and halving.\n\nYour senses seem to work roughly logarithmically too, noticing proportional changes rather than absolute ones. An extra candle brightens a dark room far more than a sunny one. The idea Napier spent twenty years refining still shapes how scientists see the world, long after its original purpose disappeared."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Using powers of 2, why does 8 × 16 equal 128?", ["Three twos and four twos make seven twos","Sixteen is the square of eight","Multiplying always means adding the two numbers","Doubling sixteen twice gives 128"], 0, "Multiplying powers of a number means adding their counts, which is the idea behind logarithms."],
    [1, "If the logarithm of 2 is about 0.301 and of 8 about 0.903, what number has a logarithm of about 1.204?", ["10","16","24","64"], 1, "Adding the logarithms gives 1.204, and the number with that logarithm is 2 × 8 = 16."],
    [2, "What change did Briggs and Napier agree would make logarithms more convenient?", ["Using base 10, so log 10 equals 1","Using tables with fewer entries","Replacing numbers with ratios","Printing tables in a Latin book"], 0, "With base 10, the logarithms of 10, 100 and 1,000 become 1, 2 and 3."],
    [3, "How does a slide rule multiply numbers?", ["By counting beads on rods","Sliding two log scales to add lengths","By turning a crank with gears","By reading a table of products"], 1, "Adding lengths on logarithmic scales is the same as multiplying the numbers."],
    [4, "What does a rise of 10 decibels mean?", ["Ten times the sound intensity","Ten more units of sound","Double the sound intensity","Ten per cent more sound"], 0, "The decibel scale is logarithmic, so equal steps mean multiplying the intensity."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A Scottish laird spent twenty years on a shortcut that engineers used for 350 years."});

WB.L("ramanujan", "maths", "intermediate", "Ramanujan: the clerk who wrote to Cambridge",
  "A largely self-taught clerk in Madras posted pages of strange formulas to a famous English mathematician. They were so astonishing that they changed both men's lives.",
  [
    ["A borrowed book", "Ramanujan taught himself mathematics from a book of results with few proofs, at the cost of his formal education.",
      "Srinivasa Ramanujan was born on 22 December 1887 in Erode, in southern India, and grew up in Kumbakonam. As a teenager he found a book by George Shoobridge Carr, a summary of several thousand mathematical results, mostly given without proofs. He worked through it alone, filling notebooks with his own discoveries.\n\nHis obsession had a cost. He won a college scholarship but neglected his other subjects, failed his exams and lost it. For several years he lived in poverty, working with little guidance. Eventually supporters who recognised his talent helped him find a job as a clerk at the Madras Port Trust in 1912, giving him a small wage and time to keep working."],
    ["The letter to Cambridge", "Hardy recognised the genius in Ramanujan's unproven formulas and brought him to Cambridge to teach him proof.",
      "In January 1913 Ramanujan wrote to Godfrey Harold Hardy, a leading mathematician at Trinity College, Cambridge, enclosing pages of formulas, many about infinite series, without proof. Hardy got many letters from cranks and was tempted to dismiss it. But he and John Littlewood realised the pages were remarkable: some results known, a few wrong, many new and astonishing. Hardy said some must be true, because no one would have had the imagination to invent them otherwise.\n\nRamanujan sailed for England in 1914. Hardy insisted on rigorous proofs, while Ramanujan reached results by intuition, saying some came from his family goddess Namagiri. Wartime Cambridge was hard for a strict vegetarian far from home, yet in 1918 he became a Fellow of the Royal Society, one of the first Indians so honoured."],
    ["The taxicab number", "Ramanujan seemed to know individual numbers the way other people know old friends.",
      "The most famous story comes from Hardy. Visiting his friend in hospital, Hardy remarked that he had arrived in taxi number 1729, which seemed a rather dull number. Ramanujan replied that, on the contrary, it was very interesting. It is the smallest number that can be written as the sum of two cubes in two different ways.\n\nCheck it: 1 cubed plus 12 cubed is 1 + 1,728, which is 1,729. And 9 cubed plus 10 cubed is 729 + 1,000, which is also 1,729. Numbers with this property are now called taxicab numbers. The story captures something real about Ramanujan, who knew individual numbers like old friends."],
    ["Breaking numbers into pieces", "Hardy and Ramanujan found a formula that estimates the number of partitions of a whole number with astonishing accuracy.",
      "One of their greatest achievements concerns partitions, the ways to write a whole number as a sum of positive whole numbers. The number 4 has five: 4, 3+1, 2+2, 2+1+1 and 1+1+1+1. Counts explode as numbers grow. The number 200 has 3,972,999,029,388, a figure Percy MacMahon worked out painstakingly by hand.\n\nHardy and Ramanujan found a formula that estimated such values with astonishing accuracy, later refined into an exact formula. Ramanujan also spotted hidden patterns. The partition counts of 4, 9, 14, 19, 24 and so on, every fifth number, are always divisible by 5. Those counts begin 5, 30, 135, 490 and 1,575."],
    ["A short life, long legacy", "Ramanujan died at 32, but his notebooks still inspire mathematics and show how easily talent can be missed.",
      "By 1917 Ramanujan was seriously ill. Doctors suspected tuberculosis, though the cause is still debated and some studies suggest a liver infection, amoebiasis. He returned to India in 1919, still working on ‘mock theta functions’, and died on 26 April 1920, aged 32. In 1976 George Andrews found a bundle of later papers, the ‘lost notebook’, in Trinity's library. Researchers spent decades proving its results, and some ideas have found uses in modern physics.\n\nHis fast formulas for pi, published in 1914, were used by computer scientists in the 1980s to compute millions of digits. India celebrates 22 December as National Mathematics Day. His story is also a warning: he was found partly by luck, through supporters and one letter that reached the right reader. Hardy wondered how much more he might have achieved with an earlier, fuller education."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Ramanujan lose his college scholarship?", ["He failed exams in his other subjects","He left college to work as a clerk","He could not afford the fees","He refused to study mathematics"], 0, "His obsession with mathematics cost him his other subjects, his exams and the scholarship."],
    [1, "What did Hardy conclude about some of Ramanujan's formulas?", ["They were copied from a textbook","They were wrong but cleverly disguised","They could be proved by a simple trick","No one would invent them if false"], 3, "Hardy judged that no one would have the imagination to invent such formulas if they were false."],
    [2, "Which pair of cube sums shows why 1729 is special?", ["1 + 1,728 and 729 + 1,000","1 + 1,000 and 729 + 1,728","1 + 729 and 1,000 + 1,728","8 + 1,728 and 729 + 1,331"], 0, "1,729 is the smallest number that is the sum of two cubes in two different ways."],
    [3, "Which pattern did Ramanujan spot in partition counts?", ["Every partition count is an even number","Counts for 4, 9, 14 and so on divide by 5","Counts double whenever the number grows","Every partition count is a prime number"], 1, "Every fifth partition count, starting at 4, is divisible by 5, such as 5, 30, 135 and 490."],
    [4, "Why is Ramanujan's story called a warning about lost talent?", ["He was found partly by chance, via one letter","He never published any of his results","His results were all later proved wrong","He refused help from Cambridge mathematicians"], 0, "His talent reached the right reader by chance, so many others may never have been found."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A clerk's letter to Cambridge held formulas so strange they had to be true."});

WB.L("fair-division", "maths", "intermediate", "I cut, you choose: the maths of fair sharing",
  "Two children and one cake have a perfect solution that is thousands of years old. Add more children and mathematicians needed until 2016 to finish the job.",
  [
    ["I cut, you choose", "Divide and choose works because the cutter gets whichever piece is left, so they have every reason to cut fairly.",
      "Two siblings, one slice of cake, and an argument brewing. The classic solution is simple: one cuts, the other chooses. The cutter has every reason to make the pieces equal in their own eyes, because they get whichever piece is left. The chooser takes the piece they prefer. Neither can reasonably complain.\n\nThe idea is ancient. In the Book of Genesis, Abraham proposes dividing the land between himself and Lot, and lets Lot choose. Mathematicians admire it because it works even when people value things differently. One sibling may care about the icing and the other about size. Nobody needs to agree on what fair means or trust anybody else's judgement."],
    ["Two kinds of fairness", "Envy-free is stricter than proportional, and the last diminisher method is proportional but not envy-free.",
      "A division is proportional if each of n people gets what they consider at least one nth of the total. It is envy-free if nobody would rather swap their share for another's. For two people these are the same. With three or more they differ: you may think you got a fair third yet feel someone else did better.\n\nIn the 1940s Hugo Steinhaus, Stefan Banach and Bronisław Knaster devised the last diminisher method. The first person cuts a slice they think is fair. Anyone who thinks it too big may trim it, and the last person to trim takes it. The rest continue with what remains. It is proportional, but not envy-free."],
    ["Three people, then many", "Envy-free sharing was solved for three people around 1960, but for any number of people only in 2016, and not practically.",
      "Around 1960 John Selfridge and John Conway each found an envy-free method for three people, now called the Selfridge–Conway procedure. Roughly, the first person cuts three equal pieces. The second trims the biggest to tie with the second biggest. The third picks first, the second picks next, and the first takes what remains. A second round shares the trimmings. Nobody prefers anyone else's total, in at most five cuts.\n\nFor four or more people it was far harder. In 1995 Steven Brams and Alan Taylor published an envy-free procedure for any number of people, but it had no upper limit on cuts. In 2016 Haris Aziz and Simon Mackenzie found a bounded method, though it needs astronomically many steps. It proves fairness is possible, not that it is practical."],
    ["Splitting the rent", "Fair division also works for rooms and rent, always allowing prices where each housemate prefers their own room.",
      "Fair division is not just about cake. Housemates sharing a place with a big sunny room, a small room and a room near the noisy road must divide a rent bill as well as the rooms. In 1999 the mathematician Francis Su published a method, building on others' work, that uses a result called Sperner's lemma. It shows there is always a way to set the rents so that each person prefers their own room at its price.\n\nOnline tools now use these ideas to split rent, chores and shared costs. Fairness flips when the thing shared is unpleasant. With chores, the cutter divides the jobs into two loads they find equally heavy, and the chooser takes the lighter one."],
    ["Fairness under the sea", "A nursery rule has become a tool of diplomacy: good mechanisms, not trust, make sharing work.",
      "The most surprising use of divide-and-choose may be international law. Under the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea, agreed in 1982, a company or country wanting to mine the deep seabed beyond national borders proposes an area large enough for two mining operations, split into two parts of similar estimated value.\n\nThe International Seabed Authority then chooses one part to reserve for itself or for developing countries, and the applicant may work the other. The applicant has every reason to divide fairly, just like the child cutting the cake. A nursery rule has become a tool of diplomacy, showing that good mechanisms, rather than trust or goodwill, can make sharing work."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why does ‘I cut, you choose’ encourage the cutter to be fair?", ["The chooser can punish an unfair cut","Cutters are always more skilled","The cutter gets whichever piece is left","Both pieces are always exactly equal"], 2, "Since the cutter takes what the chooser leaves, an even split is their best option."],
    [1, "What does it mean for a division to be envy-free?", ["Everyone gets an identical share","The shares are cut by a neutral person","Each person gets exactly one nth of the total","Nobody would rather have someone else's share"], 3, "Envy-free is stricter than proportional because it removes any wish to swap."],
    [2, "What did the 2016 result by Aziz and Mackenzie achieve?", ["A bounded envy-free method for any number of people","A practical rule for splitting a cake at parties","A proof that envy-free sharing is impossible","An envy-free method for three people only"], 0, "It showed envy-free sharing can always finish in a fixed number of steps, though far too many to use."],
    [3, "What does Su's rent method show for housemates?", ["The biggest room should cost the most","Some set of rents leaves nobody envious","Rent should be split equally","Housemates should draw lots for rooms"], 1, "A way always exists to set the rents so that nobody envies another's room at its price."],
    [4, "In deep-sea mining, why does the applicant divide the area into two parts of similar value?", ["The other party picks which part to reserve","Mining is only allowed on equal-sized areas","The applicant keeps both parts","Both parts are shared with the public"], 0, "Since the Authority chooses, the applicant has every reason to make the halves fair, as in cut and choose."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Why a fair cake split for three people took mathematicians until around 1960."});

WB.L("queueing-maths", "maths", "intermediate", "The maths of queues: why lines get long so suddenly",
  "A café that is busy most of the time can run smoothly, then a few extra customers turn it into a long wait. Queueing theory explains why, and how to fix it.",
  [
    ["Why waiting appears", "Queues form from variability in arrivals and service times, even when a server has spare capacity on average.",
      "In the early 1900s telephone companies faced an expensive puzzle: too few lines and callers heard a busy signal; too many and costly equipment sat idle. The Danish mathematician Agner Krarup Erlang, working for the Copenhagen Telephone Company, tackled it with probability. His 1909 paper began queueing theory, the mathematics of waiting. The erlang, a unit of telephone traffic, is named after him. The same maths now runs call centres, emergency departments, checkouts, traffic lights and internet servers.\n\nA coffee cart serves 30 customers an hour and 24 arrive on average. Yet customers arrive in clumps, and orders vary in complexity. Idle time in quiet moments cannot be saved for later. Variability, not just average workload, creates waiting."],
    ["The hockey stick", "Waiting stays modest until utilisation nears full capacity, then climbs steeply.",
      "In a classic model of one server with random arrivals and service times, the average wait depends on utilisation, the fraction of time the server is busy. At 50 per cent utilisation the average wait equals about one typical service time. At 80 per cent it is four service times. At 90 per cent it is nine, and at 95 per cent it is nineteen.\n\nPlotted on a graph, the line stays fairly flat, then bends sharply upward like a hockey stick. That is why a café can cope well for most of the morning, then be swamped when a few extra customers arrive. A system run near full capacity, with no slack, is fragile."],
    ["One line or many?", "Pooling several servers behind one line usually shortens and evens out the average wait.",
      "At a supermarket with a separate line at every checkout, the person in front of you has a price problem while the next line races ahead. A bank or airport instead uses one snaking queue feeding several counters, with the front person going to whichever frees up first.\n\nThe average wait is usually shorter with a single queue, and the waits are far more even. Nobody gets stuck behind one slow transaction while other servers sit idle. Mathematicians call this the benefit of pooling. Separate lines can still make sense when space is tight or when express lanes speed up small purchases, but pooling is one of the most reliable lessons of queueing theory."],
    ["Little's law", "On average, the number of items in a stable system equals the arrival rate multiplied by the time each stays.",
      "One of the most useful results is beautifully simple. Little's law, proved by John Little in 1961, says the average number of items in a stable system equals the rate at which they arrive multiplied by the average time each one stays.\n\nIf a café gets 20 customers an hour and each stays half an hour, then on average 20 × 0.5, or 10 customers are inside at any moment. The law works almost regardless of the details. It applies to shoppers, patients in a hospital ward, cars on a motorway or tasks waiting on a computer. Managers use it in reverse too: knowing how many people are typically present and how fast they arrive tells you how long each stays."],
    ["Waiting well", "Slack, smoother demand and a fair, informed wait make systems and people cope better with queues.",
      "People judge waits by how they feel. In 1985 David Maister wrote an influential essay noting that unoccupied time feels longer than occupied time, uncertain waits feel longer than known ones, unexplained waits feel longer, and so do unfair ones, such as watching someone push in. That is why theme parks post estimated waits, apps show your position in a queue, and businesses put screens where people line up.\n\nThe maths applies to your week too. A schedule packed to 95 per cent of your time collapses at the first delay. Slack is not laziness. Staggering appointments smooths out bunching, and sharing one inbox spreads work. A long line is rarely about one slow person. It is usually mathematics at work."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why can a queue form even when a server has spare capacity on average?", ["Servers always work slower when busy","Variability, and idle time cannot be banked","Customers prefer to wait together","Average capacity is always overstated"], 1, "Clumped arrivals create waiting, and the idle time during quiet moments is lost for good."],
    [1, "In the classic single-server model, how does the average wait at 90% utilisation compare with 50%?", ["About nine times as long","About twice as long","About the same","About 90% longer"], 0, "The wait rises from one service time at 50% to nine at 90%, the steep end of the hockey stick."],
    [2, "Why is a single queue feeding several counters usually fairer than separate lines?", ["It makes each transaction faster","It evens out waits between customers","It needs fewer staff in every case","It removes the need for express lanes"], 1, "Pooling means the next person goes to whichever server frees up first, reducing both the average and the unevenness."],
    [3, "A clinic sees 6 patients an hour and each stays 2 hours on average. By Little's law, how many are there on average?", ["3","8","12","24"], 2, "Arrival rate times average stay is 6 × 2, so about 12 patients are present at any time."],
    [4, "Why does a week scheduled to 95 per cent of your time tend to go wrong?", ["Small delays snowball with no slack","Busy people always work faster","Appointments never run to time","People forget what they scheduled"], 0, "Near full capacity there is no slack to absorb variability, just as the hockey-stick curve predicts."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A café with spare capacity can still have a long queue. The maths explains why."});

WB.L("calculus-without-fear", "maths", "advanced", "Calculus without fear: the maths of change",
  "Calculus has a fearsome reputation, but its two big ideas can be grasped with a speedometer and an odometer. Its invention also sparked one of the bitterest feuds in science.",
  [
    ["Two simple questions", "Calculus answers two questions: how fast something is changing right now, and how much has built up over time.",
      "Strip away the symbols and calculus asks two questions. How fast is something changing at this exact moment? And if you know how fast it is changing, how much has it added up to over time? Every car shows both ideas. The speedometer reports how fast your distance is changing right now. The odometer adds up all those changing speeds into a total distance. The first idea is differentiation, which gives a derivative. The second is integration, which gives an integral. Together they sit at the heart of physics, engineering, economics and biology, anywhere things change smoothly. Ordinary arithmetic copes with fixed values. Calculus copes with constant change."],
    ["Ancient near-misses", "Zeno's paradoxes showed that infinitely small steps are tricky, and Archimedes trapped answers between ever-finer approximations.",
      "Long before anyone named calculus, the Greeks wrestled with its core difficulty. Around the fifth century BCE, Zeno of Elea argued that swift Achilles can never catch a tortoise with a head start, since each time he reaches where it was, it has moved on. How can infinitely many steps add up to a finite result? Today we can answer: a half, a quarter, an eighth and so on forever totals exactly 1. In the third century BCE, Archimedes squeezed a circle between polygons inside and outside it. With 96 sides, he showed pi lies between 223/71 and 22/7, about 3.1408 and 3.1429. He used similar reasoning for the area under a parabola and the volume of a sphere. He lacked a general method."],
    ["Newton versus Leibniz", "Newton and Leibniz invented calculus independently, and a bitter dispute over who came first followed.",
      "In the mid-1660s, with Cambridge closed by plague, young Isaac Newton developed his method of fluxions but kept it largely to himself. In Germany, Gottfried Leibniz developed his own version in the 1670s and published first, in 1684. A bitter priority dispute followed, with Newton's supporters accusing Leibniz of plagiarism. In 1712 the Royal Society, which Newton led as president, ruled in Newton's favour in a report he secretly drafted much of himself. Historians now agree the two men invented calculus independently. Leibniz's notation, such as dy/dx and the long S for an integral, proved clearer and won out. British mathematicians, loyal to Newton's dots, fell behind continental Europe for decades."],
    ["Zooming and slicing", "A derivative zooms in to find the rate of change at an instant; an integral adds up thin slices to find a total.",
      "Drop a ball and it falls about 4.9 × t² metres in t seconds, ignoring air resistance. How fast is it moving at exactly one second? From 1 to 1.1 seconds, the average speed is 10.29 metres per second. From 1 to 1.01, it is about 9.85. As the interval shrinks, the average closes in on exactly 9.8. That limit is the derivative: the slope of a curve at a single point.\n\nIntegration runs the other way. A steady 60 km/h for two hours is a rectangle on a speed graph, 60 high and 2 wide, so 120 kilometres. For varying speed, slice the area into thin strips and add them up. Infinitely thin strips give the exact answer. Engineers use this for forces on dams and volumes of fuel tanks."],
    ["Why calculus took over", "The fundamental theorem links the two halves of calculus, and nineteenth-century limits finally made it rigorous.",
      "The deepest discovery is that the two halves undo each other. Integrate a speed to get distance, differentiate that, and you get the speed back. This is the fundamental theorem of calculus. Isaac Barrow, Newton's teacher, had glimpsed it, but Newton and Leibniz made it a working method. Instead of adding thousands of strips, you find a formula whose derivative matches your curve, and the area follows almost instantly.\n\nYet the foundations were shaky. In 1734 George Berkeley mocked vanishing quantities as ‘ghosts of departed quantities’. In the nineteenth century, Cauchy and Weierstrass rebuilt calculus on precisely defined limits, answering Zeno at last. Today it underpins weather forecasts, rocket trajectories, medical scanners and AI training, which follows derivatives downhill to reduce errors."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "In the car picture of calculus, what does the odometer represent?", ["Finding speed at a single instant","Measuring how quickly speed is changing","Adding up changing speeds into a total distance","Estimating fuel used per kilometre"], 2, "The odometer accumulates speed over time, which is what integration does; the speedometer shows differentiation."],
    [1, "What does adding a half, a quarter, an eighth and so on forever show?", ["Fractions can never be added together","An infinite sum can have a finite value","Achilles really cannot catch the tortoise","Infinite sums always grow without limit"], 1, "The total approaches exactly 1, which is how we answer Zeno's worry about infinite steps."],
    [2, "How do historians now view the invention of calculus?", ["Newton invented it and Leibniz copied it","Leibniz invented it and Newton copied it","Archimedes had already completed it","Newton and Leibniz each invented it independently"], 3, "Both men reached calculus on their own, though their supporters fought bitterly over priority."],
    [3, "As the time interval around one second shrinks, what happens to the falling ball's average speed?", ["It approaches 9.8 m/s","It rises without limit","It falls towards zero","It stays at 10.29 m/s"], 0, "A derivative is the limit that average speed approaches as the interval shrinks towards zero."],
    [4, "What does the fundamental theorem of calculus say?", ["Every curve is a straight line up close","Infinitely small quantities are always zero","Differentiation and integration undo each other","Calculus only works for circles and spheres"], 2, "Because the two operations are opposites, hard area problems can often be solved by finding a formula instead of adding strips."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"You can grasp calculus with a car dashboard. Its inventors spent years at war."});

